issues.problematic_code_snippet:
class WebServer: WebServerProtocol, @unchecked Sendable {
    static let sharedInstance = WebServer()

    private let logger: Logger

    let server = GCDWebServer()

func handleServerTrust(
    challenge: URLAuthenticationChallenge,
    dispatchQueue: DispatchQueueInterface,
    completionHandler: @escaping @MainActor (URLSession.AuthChallengeDisposition, URLCredential?) -> Void
) {
    dispatchQueue.async {
        let origin = "\(challenge.protectionSpace.host):\(challenge.protectionSpace.port)"

        guard let trust = challenge.protectionSpace.serverTrust,
              let cert = SecTrustCopyCertificateChain(trust) as? [SecCertificate],
              self.profile.certStore.containsCertificate(cert[0], forOrigin: origin)
        else {
            completionHandler(.performDefaultHandling, nil)
            return
        }

public func ensureMainThread<T>(execute work: @escaping @MainActor () -> T) -> T? {
    if Thread.isMainThread {
        return MainActor.assumeIsolated {
            return work()
        }
    } else {
        DispatchQueue.main.async {
            _ = work()
        }
        return nil
    }
}

func throttle(completion: @escaping @MainActor () -> Void) {
    lastExecutionTime = Date()
    guard threshold <= 0 || lastExecutionTime.timeIntervalSinceNow < -threshold else { return }
    DispatchQueue.main.async(execute: completion)
}

findings.body:
In `throttle`, `lastExecutionTime = Date()` now runs before the guard that compares `lastExecutionTime.timeIntervalSinceNow` against `-threshold`. The guard therefore always sees a value near zero. With a positive threshold (the default is 0.35s), `~0 < -threshold` is false and the function returns without running `completion`. Debounced work never executes.

The existing `MainThreadThrottlerTests.testSecondCallAfterDelayThresholdCallsBothClosures` and `testMultipleFastConsecutiveCallsAreThrottledAndExecutedAtMostOneTime` expect the first completion to fire, so they will time out on this change. Only the `threshold <= 0` path still works.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Utils/MainThreadThrottler.swift" lines="25-29"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The original order was: read the previous timestamp in the guard, then update it only if the call passes. Restore that order.

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Client/Utils/MainThreadThrottler.swift" lines="26-28"}
        guard threshold <= 0 || Date().timeIntervalSince(lastExecutionTime) > threshold else { return }
        lastExecutionTime = Date()
        DispatchQueue.main.async(execute: completion)

`handleServerTrust` runs its body inside `dispatchQueue.async`, which is a background queue. Before this PR, the rejection path wrapped `completionHandler(.performDefaultHandling, nil)` in `ensureMainThread`, the same way the accept path still does. This PR removes that wrapper, so WebKit's `@MainActor` challenge completion now runs on a background thread on the rejection path.

The two branches now disagree about the thread. The `@MainActor` completion is also called from a non-isolated closure, which may fail to compile under Swift 6 strict concurrency. Restoring the `ensureMainThread` wrapper, or moving the whole completion onto the main actor, keeps the two paths consistent.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/BrowserViewController/Extensions/BrowserViewController+WebViewDelegates.swift" lines="1334-1351"}

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/BrowserViewController/Extensions/BrowserViewController+WebViewDelegates.swift" lines="1343-1343"}
                ensureMainThread {
                    completionHandler(.performDefaultHandling, nil)
                }

The generic overload changed from returning `Void` to returning `T?`. It executes `work` synchronously on the main thread and returns the value. Off the main thread it schedules `work` asynchronously and returns `nil`, even though `work` ran. A caller that reads the result gets a value on one thread and `nil` on another, and nothing in the type signature says so.

This change is also not part of the stated purpose of the PR, which is removing `@Sendable`. Two more concerns: passing a closure that returns a non-`Void` value now resolves to this overload and can produce unused-result warnings, and `MainActor.assumeIsolated` may require its result type to be `Sendable`, which the unconstrained `T` does not guarantee. Split this out into its own change. If the value is needed, make the API explicitly synchronous-only, for example by asserting `Thread.isMainThread` instead of returning `nil`.

::diff{file="BrowserKit/Sources/Common/Utilities/DispatchQueueHelper.swift" lines="22-32"}

The PR updates mock signatures but adds no tests. The throttler regression above would have been caught by the existing `MainThreadThrottlerTests`, so the PR was probably not run against them. There is also no test for the generic `ensureMainThread` return value, which now behaves differently on and off the main thread, or for the server-trust rejection path that changed threads.

Add a test that asserts the throttler's completion runs for the first call when the threshold is positive. Add one that asserts the rejection path of `handleServerTrust` calls the completion on the main thread.

